 The first-boot ceremony on the GOP framebuffer (B2.2 + B2.3). The
 flow mirrors FirstBoot's phase model -- welcome, identity, upstream,
 complete -- rendered with GopDraw and driven by GopText fields over
 whichever keyboard the machine has: PS/2 by IRQ, or USB HID by the
 pump the context carries (wz-kbd), because on the Option A boot path
 firmware is gone: no ConOut, no ConIn, and on most modern machines
 no PS/2 controller either.

 Identity follows the IdentityManager recipe: entropy is hashed with
 SHA-256 into an Ed25519 seed, the public key's hash is the
 fingerprint, and the private seed is wrapped with AES-256-CBC under
 an HKDF key derived from the passphrase. Entropy sources are the
 device seed cell (32 bytes at 30576, filled by the Option A stub from
 RDRAND where the processor has it and from four rotated TSC samples
 where it does not -- weak in that case, but never the all-zero cell
 this keygen once mixed in), the typed random sentence, the
 passphrase, and the timer
 tick count sampled after typing -- keystroke timing the machine
 cannot predict. The wrapped key lives only in memory: persistence to
 the stick is B3.

 Every waiting loop is a self-recursive act tail (constant stack under
 the compiler's TCO) whose idle path allocates nothing; the hosting
 program compiles with the pet flag like every boot-path poll loop.

 We say:

Section: Context

  WzCtx = record {
    wz-base : Integer,
    wz-w : Integer,
    wz-h : Integer,
    wz-stride : Integer,
    wz-font : CbfFont,
    wz-scale : Integer,
    wz-kbd : UsbKbd
  }

  wz-col-bg : Integer = 1710638
  wz-col-title : Integer = 65535
  wz-col-text : Integer = 9474192
  wz-col-bright : Integer = 16777215
  wz-col-green : Integer = 65280
  wz-col-field : Integer = 3421236
  wz-col-warn : Integer = 16755200

  wz-left : WzCtx -> Integer
  wz-left (c) = (c.wz-w - 480 * c.wz-scale) / 2

  wz-top : WzCtx -> Integer
  wz-top (c) = (c.wz-h - 320 * c.wz-scale) / 2

  wz-row : WzCtx, Integer -> Integer
  wz-row (c) (i) = wz-top c + i * 24 * c.wz-scale

  wz-clear : WzCtx -> Integer
  wz-clear (c) = gop-fill-rect (c.wz-base) (c.wz-stride) 0 0 (c.wz-w) (c.wz-h) wz-col-bg

  wz-text : WzCtx, Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  wz-text (c) (row) (s) (color) =
    gop-draw-text (c.wz-base) (c.wz-stride) (c.wz-font) (wz-left c) (wz-row c row) s color (c.wz-scale) 0

  wz-field : WzCtx, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> GtField
  wz-field (c) (row) (wchars) (masked) = GtField {
    gf-base = c.wz-base,
    gf-stride = c.wz-stride,
    gf-font = c.wz-font,
    gf-x = wz-left c,
    gf-y = wz-row c row,
    gf-wchars = wchars,
    gf-scale = c.wz-scale,
    gf-fg = wz-col-bright,
    gf-bg = wz-col-field,
    gf-cursor = wz-col-green,
    gf-masked = masked
  }

Section: Key Wait

 The wait polls through kbd-take so a USB keyboard's Enter arrives
 exactly like a PS/2 one; with no USB keyboard connected the pump is
 a flag test and this is gk-take in a loop, unchanged.

 This wait is also the keyboard proof gate, because it is the one
 place the boot stops and asks a human to press something. A key --
 any key, not just the Enter this screen wants -- proves the keyboard
 delivers, and restarts the window. Silence proves nothing on its
 own: a machine whose keyboard is simply unattended looks exactly
 like a machine whose USB HID endpoint enumerated and never spoke.
 The gate treats them the same on purpose, because the remedy is
 harmless to the first and is the only remedy for the second: after
 a bounded silence the controller goes back to firmware (kbd-handback)
 and its legacy emulation answers instead. The cost is the USB boot
 stick, which shares the controller; a machine nobody can type on
 could not have used it anyway.

 The window is measured on the CMOS real-time clock, NOT on the PIT
 tick, and that is the whole lesson of the first attempt. The tick
 cell (28672) is filled by the timer interrupt, so it only advances
 on a machine whose PIC and PIT survived ExitBootServices and whose
 IDT is live. The first build of this gate trusted it, fired
 perfectly under OVMF, and on the ASUS -- the one machine it was
 written for -- never fired at all: the elapsed time was permanently
 zero, so the wait for a key that could never come was itself
 unbounded. The RTC is read straight off ports 0x70/0x71 with no
 interrupt, no PIC, and no handler in the path; a machine that can
 boot at all has one. The gate now counts the seconds it sees the
 RTC turn over, which is a clock the firmware cannot take away.

 Both clocks are reported on the glass while the gate waits, because
 a boot that fails to fire must say WHY: if pit stays 0 while rtc
 climbs, the timer interrupt is dead on that machine and everything
 else built on the tick (the watchdog, preemption, any timeout) is
 dead with it. That is a finding, not a nuisance.

 The gate fires at most once -- the handback records its own
 decision -- so a firmware that refuses to take the controller back
 is not asked twice.

 wz-wait-enter-from tail-calls ITSELF so the compiler's TCO turns the
 poll into a jump; the gate is a helper returning an integer, exactly
 as the menu's dispatchers are, because mutual recursion would grow
 the stack until the machine died waiting for a keystroke.

 The gate reports its own vitals, unconditionally, on a fixed spin
 interval -- not when a second turns over, and not when a key
 arrives. The first two builds of this gate each depended on a clock
 to say anything at all, and each went silent on the one machine it
 was written for: a screen that prints nothing cannot be
 distinguished from a loop that is not running, an RTC that never
 ticks, or a keyboard streaming garbage that resets the window every
 iteration. Those are three different faults and they looked
 identical. The spin counter is the one number that cannot lie: if it
 climbs, the loop is alive, and every other number on the line is
 then worth reading.

 The window resets only on a real MAKE code (1..0x7F). A break code,
 and in particular the i8042's power-on 0xFA acknowledgement -- which
 lands in the mailbox on a machine with an i8042 and no PS/2 keyboard
 -- must not count as proof that a human is typing, or a board that
 emits one holds the gate open forever and never falls back.

 The spin backstop is for ONE case, and it must never pre-empt a
 working clock: a machine where neither the timer interrupt NOR the
 real-time clock advances at all. Written as a plain spin budget it
 fired within a second or two on real silicon -- that CPU spins tens
 of millions of times a second, so sixty million spins is not "a long
 wait", it is an instant -- and it handed the controller back before
 the thirty-second window had even begun, switching off the very
 keyboard pump whose silence it was supposed to be measuring. A
 budget in spins is not a duration. It only becomes one when there is
 nothing else to count by.

 So the backstop now requires that the RTC has NEVER moved: elapsed
 still zero after a spin count no working machine reaches without the
 clock turning over once. On a board whose RTC ticks -- which is every
 board we have met -- the thirty seconds are the only thing that can
 fire this gate.

 The window is THIRTY seconds and it announces itself, because the
 handback is not free: it costs the USB stick, and on a machine whose
 firmware offers no legacy emulation it costs the USB keyboard too.
 A ten-second window fired while a human was still reading the
 welcome screen -- caught in the emulator, where the fallback has
 nothing to fall back TO, and a working keyboard was destroyed by the
 mechanism meant to save it. So the machine now says what it is about
 to do, counts down where the operator can see it, and any keystroke
 at all calls the whole thing off. A person who has not touched a key
 in half a minute, on a screen that says "Press Enter to begin", does
 not have a working keyboard.

  wz-gate-secs : Integer = 30
  wz-noclock-spins : Integer = 500000000
  wz-note-every : Integer = 262144

  wz-pit : Integer -> Integer
  wz-pit (d) = peek-qword 28672 0

 The clock is read through GopRtc, which waits out Update-In-Progress,
 reads twice, and rejects anything that is not a second. Reading the
 seconds register raw -- as this gate did, for three builds -- returns
 garbage while the part is updating, and a poll loop reading millions of
 times a second lands in that window constantly. The window then counted
 thirty "seconds" in microseconds, handed the controller back before the
 countdown could be seen, and switched off the keyboard pump whose
 silence it was measuring. An unreadable clock is reported as -1 and
 changes nothing: it is not a tick, and it never becomes one.

 A status row is cleared across the WHOLE panel, not across the text
 column: these lines are longer than the ceremony's prose, and a
 clear sized to the column leaves the tail of the previous, longer
 line standing beside the new one.

  wz-clear-row : WzCtx, Integer -> Integer
  wz-clear-row (c) (row) =
    gop-fill-rect (c.wz-base) (c.wz-stride) 0 (wz-row c row) (c.wz-w) (18 * c.wz-scale) wz-col-bg

  wz-countdown : Integer -> Text
  wz-countdown (elapsed) =
    let n = wz-gate-secs - elapsed
    in if n < 0 then "0" else show n

  wz-status-line : Integer -> Text
  wz-status-line (elapsed) =
    if kbd-released 0 /= 0 then "Handed back to firmware. Try the keyboard now."
    else "No key -- firmware keyboard in " & wz-countdown elapsed & "s. Press a key."

  wz-vitals : WzCtx, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wz-vitals (c) (spins) (elapsed) (raw) (sc) =
    let z = wz-clear-row c 13
    in let y = wz-clear-row c 14
    in let t = wz-text c 13 (wz-status-line elapsed) wz-col-warn
    in wz-text c 14 ("spin=" & show (spins / 1024) & "k s=" & show elapsed & " rtc=" & show raw & " pit=" & show (wz-pit 0) & " sc=" & show sc) wz-col-text

  wz-gate-fire : WzCtx -> Integer
  wz-gate-fire (c) = kbd-handback 0

  wz-real-key : Integer -> Boolean
  wz-real-key (sc) = sc > 0 & sc < 128

  wz-wait-enter : WzCtx -> [Device.Port] Integer
  wz-wait-enter (c) = act
    r <- rtc-seconds 0
    wz-wait-enter-from c r 0 0 0
  end

 An unreadable second (-1) is carried through as no change at all: prev is
 left standing, elapsed does not move, and the loop simply asks again. A
 clock that cannot be read is not a clock that has ticked.

  wz-wait-enter-from : WzCtx, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  wz-wait-enter-from (c) (prev) (elapsed) (spins) (last) = act
    sc <- kbd-take (c.wz-kbd)
    now <- rtc-seconds 0
    let seen = if wz-real-key sc then sc else last
    in let good = now >= 0
    in let tick = good & now /= prev
    in let e2 = if tick then elapsed + 1 else elapsed
    in let p2 = if good then now else prev
    in let note = if int-mod spins wz-note-every == 0 then wz-vitals c spins e2 now seen else 0
    in let fire = if kbd-released 0 /= 0 then 0
      else if wz-real-key sc then 0
      else if e2 >= wz-gate-secs then wz-gate-fire c
      else if e2 == 0 & spins >= wz-noclock-spins then wz-gate-fire c
      else 0
    in if sc == 28 then 0
    else if wz-real-key sc then wz-wait-enter-from c p2 0 0 seen
    else wz-wait-enter-from c p2 e2 (spins + 1) seen
  end

Section: Welcome

  wz-welcome : WzCtx -> [Device.Port] Integer
  wz-welcome (c) =
    let d0 = wz-clear c
    in let t0 = wz-text c 0 "Welcome to Codex" wz-col-title
    in let t1 = wz-text c 2 "A self-sustaining operating system, compiler, and" wz-col-text
    in let t2 = wz-text c 3 "development environment. Everything on this device" wz-col-text
    in let t3 = wz-text c 4 "was compiled by itself." wz-col-text
    in let t4 = wz-text c 6 "This is first boot. We will:" wz-col-text
    in let t5 = wz-text c 7 "  1. Generate your identity (Ed25519 keypair)" wz-col-text
    in let t6 = wz-text c 8 "  2. Configure an upstream server (optional)" wz-col-text
    in let t7 = wz-text c 9 "  3. Choose your preferred interface" wz-col-text
    in let t8 = wz-text c 11 "Press Enter to begin." wz-col-green
    in let t9 = wz-text c 13 ("Keyboard: " & (if (c.wz-kbd).uk-ok then "USB HID" else "PS/2")) wz-col-text
    in wz-wait-enter c

Section: Passphrase

 The passphrase protects the private key. Two masked entries must
 match and reach four characters; a failed attempt redraws the screen
 with the reason and starts over -- the retry is a tail self-call, so
 a stubborn typist costs no stack.

  wz-passphrase : WzCtx, Text -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] GtLine
  wz-passphrase (c) (note) = act
    d0 <- wz-clear c
    t0 <- wz-text c 0 "Your Identity" wz-col-title
    t1 <- wz-text c 2 "Choose a passphrase (4+ characters). It encrypts the" wz-col-text
    t2 <- wz-text c 3 "private key; without it the key cannot be used." wz-col-text
    t3 <- if text-length note > 0 then wz-text c 10 note wz-col-warn else 0
    t4 <- wz-text c 5 "Passphrase:" wz-col-text
    p1 <- gt-input (wz-field c 6 32 True) (c.wz-kbd)
    t5 <- wz-text c 8 "Confirm:" wz-col-text
    p2 <- gt-input (wz-field c 9 32 True) (c.wz-kbd)
    if p1.gl-text == p2.gl-text then
      if text-length (p1.gl-text) >= 4 then GtLine { gl-text = p1.gl-text, gl-entropy = p1.gl-entropy * 31 + p2.gl-entropy }
      else wz-passphrase c "Too short - use four or more characters."
    else wz-passphrase c "The passphrases differ - try again."
  end

Section: Entropy

  wz-entropy : WzCtx -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] GtLine
  wz-entropy (c) = act
    d0 <- wz-clear c
    t0 <- wz-text c 0 "Entropy" wz-col-title
    t1 <- wz-text c 2 "Type a random sentence. Its characters and your typing" wz-col-text
    t2 <- wz-text c 3 "time are mixed into the key material." wz-col-text
    s <- gt-input (wz-field c 5 48 False) (c.wz-kbd)
    s
  end

Section: Upstream

  wz-upstream : WzCtx -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] GtLine
  wz-upstream (c) = act
    d0 <- wz-clear c
    t0 <- wz-text c 0 "Upstream Server (optional)" wz-col-title
    t1 <- wz-text c 2 "For complex tasks a local agent can escalate to a" wz-col-text
    t2 <- wz-text c 3 "full-size model on a remote server. Enter its address," wz-col-text
    t3 <- wz-text c 4 "or press Enter alone to skip." wz-col-text
    s <- gt-input (wz-field c 6 48 False) (c.wz-kbd)
    s
  end

Section: Key Generation

  WzIdentity = record {
    wid-fingerprint : Text,
    wid-public : List Integer,
    wid-wrapped : List Integer,
    wid-salt : List Integer,
    wid-iv : List Integer
  }

  wz-device-seed-addr : Integer = 30576

  wz-peek-bytes : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  wz-peek-bytes (addr) (len) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else wz-peek-bytes addr len (i + 1) (list-push acc (peek-byte addr i))

  wz-text-bytes : Text, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  wz-text-bytes (s) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= text-length s then acc
    else wz-text-bytes s (i + 1) (list-push acc (to-unicode (char-code (char-at s i))))

  wz-words-to-bytes : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  wz-words-to-bytes (words) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length words then acc
    else let w = list-at words i
    in wz-words-to-bytes words (i + 1) (acc & [bit-and (bit-shru w 24) 255, bit-and (bit-shru w 16) 255, bit-and (bit-shru w 8) 255, bit-and w 255])

  wz-take : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  wz-take (src) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else wz-take src n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at src i))

  wz-tick-bytes : List Integer
  wz-tick-bytes =
    let t = peek-qword 28672 0
    in [bit-and t 255, bit-and (bit-shru t 8) 255, bit-and (bit-shru t 16) 255, bit-and (bit-shru t 24) 255]

  wz-hkdf-info : List Integer
  wz-hkdf-info = wz-text-bytes "codex-identity-v1" 0 []

  wz-int-bytes : Integer -> List Integer
  wz-int-bytes (v) =
    [bit-and v 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 16) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 24) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 32) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 40) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 48) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 56) 255]

  wz-keygen : Text, Text, Integer -> WzIdentity
  wz-keygen (passphrase) (sentence) (typing-ent) =
    let dev = wz-peek-bytes wz-device-seed-addr 32 0 []
    in let mix = dev & wz-text-bytes sentence 0 [] & wz-text-bytes passphrase 0 [] & wz-tick-bytes & wz-int-bytes typing-ent
    in let seed = wz-words-to-bytes (sha256 mix) 0 []
    in let pub = ed25519-public-key seed
    in let fp = sha256-to-hex (sha256 pub)
    in let salt = wz-take (wz-words-to-bytes (sha256 (dev & wz-tick-bytes & [1])) 0 []) 16 0 []
    in let iv = wz-take (wz-words-to-bytes (sha256 (dev & wz-tick-bytes & [2])) 0 []) 16 0 []
    in let dk = wz-words-to-bytes (hkdf salt (wz-text-bytes passphrase 0 []) wz-hkdf-info 32) 0 []
    in let wrapped = aes256-cbc-encrypt (aes256-pkcs7-pad seed) dk iv
    in WzIdentity {
      wid-fingerprint = fp,
      wid-public = pub,
      wid-wrapped = wrapped,
      wid-salt = salt,
      wid-iv = iv
    }

Section: Persistence

 Serialize the identity into a 512-byte cluster buffer and write it to
 the stick as IDENTITY.DAT, so a later boot can unlock instead of running
 the wizard again. The layout is fixed and self-describing: a magic, a
 version, then the salt, iv, public key, and the passphrase-wrapped
 private seed with its length. Only the wrapped key is secret, and it
 never leaves memory unencrypted -- what lands on disk is exactly what
 the wizard already computed.

  wz-id-magic0 : Integer = 67
  wz-id-magic1 : Integer = 73
  wz-id-magic2 : Integer = 68
  wz-id-magic3 : Integer = 78

  wz-put-list : Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wz-put-list (buf) (off) (xs) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let w = poke-byte buf (off + i) (list-at xs i)
    in wz-put-list buf off xs (i + 1) n

  wz-put-u32 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wz-put-u32 (buf) (off) (v) =
    let a = poke-byte buf off (bit-and v 255)
    in let b = poke-byte buf (off + 1) (bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255)
    in let c = poke-byte buf (off + 2) (bit-and (bit-shru v 16) 255)
    in poke-byte buf (off + 3) (bit-and (bit-shru v 24) 255)

  wz-serialize-identity : WzIdentity, Integer -> Integer
  wz-serialize-identity (wid) (buf) =
    let m0 = poke-byte buf 0 wz-id-magic0
    in let m1 = poke-byte buf 1 wz-id-magic1
    in let m2 = poke-byte buf 2 wz-id-magic2
    in let m3 = poke-byte buf 3 wz-id-magic3
    in let v = wz-put-u32 buf 4 1
    in let salt = wz-put-list buf 8 (wid.wid-salt) 0 16
    in let iv = wz-put-list buf 24 (wid.wid-iv) 0 16
    in let pub = wz-put-list buf 40 (wid.wid-public) 0 32
    in let wl = wz-put-u32 buf 72 (list-length (wid.wid-wrapped))
    in let wr = wz-put-list buf 76 (wid.wid-wrapped) 0 (list-length (wid.wid-wrapped))
    in 76 + list-length (wid.wid-wrapped)

 The 8.3 name "IDENTITYDAT" as 11 space-padded bytes.

  wz-id-name : Integer
  wz-id-name =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let adv = __heap-advance 16
    in let w = wz-name-bytes b "IDENTITYDAT" 0
    in b

  wz-name-bytes : Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  wz-name-bytes (buf) (s) (i) =
    if i >= text-length s then 0
    else let w = poke-byte buf i (to-unicode (char-code (char-at s i)))
    in wz-name-bytes buf s (i + 1)

 Only ever write to the disk we booted from. The AHCI driver picks the
 first SATA disk it finds, which on a real machine with internal drives
 is NOT the boot medium -- so an unguarded write could land on the wrong
 disk. The boot medium is the one carrying our own CODEX.CDX; if the
 mounted volume has no CODEX.CDX, decline rather than risk another disk.
 (On real hardware the boot stick is USB mass storage, which these
 AHCI/IDE drivers cannot reach at all -- so the mount fails first and
 this guard is belt-and-suspenders until the USB stack lands.)

  wz-save-identity : WzIdentity -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  wz-save-identity (wid) = act
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    if m.gm-ok == False then False
    else act
      seed-size <- gfat-file-size (m.gm-vol) "CODEX.CDX"
      if seed-size == 0 then False
      else act
        let buf = __heap-save
        in let adv = __heap-advance 512
        in let z = wz-zero buf 0 512
        in let len = wz-serialize-identity wid buf
        in gfat-write-small-file (m.gm-vol) wz-id-name buf len
      end
    end
  end

  wz-zero : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wz-zero (buf) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let w = poke-byte buf i 0
    in wz-zero buf (i + 1) n

  wz-identity-present : [Device.Port] Boolean
  wz-identity-present = act
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    if m.gm-ok == False then False
    else act
      sz <- gfat-file-size (m.gm-vol) "IDENTITY.DAT"
      sz > 0
    end
  end

Section: Complete

  wz-complete : WzCtx, WzIdentity, Text, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  wz-complete (c) (wid) (up) (saved) =
    let d0 = wz-clear c
    in let t0 = wz-text c 0 "Identity Created" wz-col-title
    in let t1 = wz-text c 2 ("Fingerprint: " & text-take (wid.wid-fingerprint) 32) wz-col-green
    in let t2 = wz-text c 3 ("             " & text-drop (wid.wid-fingerprint) 32) wz-col-green
    in let t3 = wz-text c 5 ("Upstream: " & (if text-length up == 0 then "(none)" else up)) wz-col-text
    in let t4 = wz-text c 7 "The private key is wrapped under your passphrase." wz-col-text
    in let t5 = if saved then wz-text c 8 "Saved to the stick as IDENTITY.DAT." wz-col-green
                else wz-text c 8 "Could not save to the stick." wz-col-warn
    in let t6 = wz-text c 10 "Press Enter to choose your interface." wz-col-green
    in wz-wait-enter c

Section: Flow

 A returning boot: a stick that already carries an identity greets you
 and skips generation. Unlocking the key -- re-deriving the HKDF key from
 the passphrase and decrypting the wrapped seed -- is the next rung
 (B3.6); for now a saved identity is detected and acknowledged.

  wz-welcome-back : WzCtx -> [Device.Port] Integer
  wz-welcome-back (c) =
    let d0 = wz-clear c
    in let t0 = wz-text c 0 "Welcome Back" wz-col-title
    in let t1 = wz-text c 2 "This stick already carries an identity (IDENTITY.DAT)." wz-col-text
    in let t2 = wz-text c 3 "Unlocking it with your passphrase arrives with B3.6." wz-col-text
    in let t3 = wz-text c 5 "Press Enter to choose your interface." wz-col-green
    in wz-wait-enter c

 Boot-diag trace lines (temporary, real-hardware bring-up): same
 bottom-region geometry as GopBoot's boot-trace, rows 4 and up, so
 on a machine with no serial the last line standing names the
 wizard step that died. Each screen's clear wipes them; a death
 mid-clear leaves them readable below the partial fill.

  wz-trace : WzCtx, Integer, Text -> Integer
  wz-trace (c) (row) (t) =
    let y = (c.wz-h) / 2 + 80 + row * 14
    in let bg = gop-fill-rect (c.wz-base) (c.wz-stride) 8 y 400 12 0
    in gop-draw-text (c.wz-base) (c.wz-stride) (c.wz-font) 8 y t #00FF00 1 0

  wizard-run : WzCtx -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] Text
  wizard-run (c) = act
    let t0 = wz-trace c 4 "wz: detect..."
    in act
      present <- wz-identity-present
      t1 <- wz-trace c 5 (if present then "wz: returning..." else "wz: fresh, welcome...")
      if present then act
        wb <- wz-welcome-back c
        let r = "returning"
        in r
      end
      else act
        w0 <- wz-welcome c
        t2 <- wz-trace c 6 "wz: welcome up, wait Enter"
        pp <- wz-passphrase c ""
        t3 <- wz-trace c 6 "wz: entropy screen"
        sent <- wz-entropy c
        t4 <- wz-trace c 6 "wz: upstream screen"
        up <- wz-upstream c
        t5 <- wz-trace c 6 "wz: keygen..."
        wid <- wz-keygen (pp.gl-text) (sent.gl-text) (pp.gl-entropy * 2654435761 + sent.gl-entropy * 40503 + up.gl-entropy)
        t6 <- wz-trace c 6 "wz: save identity..."
        saved <- wz-save-identity wid
        done <- wz-complete c wid (up.gl-text) saved
        let fp = wid.wid-fingerprint
        in fp
      end
    end
  end

